<QuestionLayout
  title="How do I cancel a running model.execute call in Traverse?"
  description="Pass an AbortSignal on traverse-embedder-web@0.17.1, or set ExactModelHostConnector.cancel on traverse-runtime 0.17.1. A browser Worker and wasmtime stop the guest during the call."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/how-do-i-cancel-a-running-model-execute-call-in-traverse.html"
  category="Runtime and architecture"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> on npm <code>traverse-embedder-web@0.17.1</code>, pass an <code>AbortSignal</code> to <code>ExactModelBrowserHost.execute</code> and call <code>abort()</code>. In a browser Worker the host terminates that Worker and the call fails with <code>cancelled</code>. On crates.io <code>traverse-runtime</code> 0.17.1, set <code>ExactModelHostConnector.cancel</code> to <code>true</code> while <code>invoke</code> is running. Wasmtime stops the guest at its next epoch check. The walk-through is <a href="/blog/stop-a-model-call-mid-run.html">Stop a model.execute call while it is still running</a>.</p>

  <h2>Browser</h2>
  <p><code>host</code> is an <code>ExactModelBrowserHost</code> from <code>traverse-embedder-web@0.17.1</code>. <code>model_ref</code> and <code>input_ref</code> are the pin and the staged input you already registered.</p>
  <pre><code>{browserSnippet}</code></pre>
  <p>If the signal is already aborted, the published package throws <code>ExactModelError</code> with code <code>cancelled</code> and message <code>model.execute cancelled before invoke</code>. The guest does not start. If the signal aborts while the guest is in its Worker, the host terminates that Worker, drops the package snapshot, and throws <code>cancelled</code> with message <code>model.execute cancelled mid-run</code>. The next call spawns a fresh Worker.</p>
  <p>The Worker file ships in the npm package as <code>dist/modelWorker.js</code>. Pass <code>createWorker</code> when your bundler does not emit it. The field list and the Worker rules are in <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/blob/v0.17.1/docs/exact-ref-model-execution.md">docs/exact-ref-model-execution.md</a> at tag v0.17.1.</p>

  <h2>Native wasmtime</h2>
  <pre><code>{nativeSnippet}</code></pre>
  <p><code>host</code> is <code>ExactModelHostConnector</code> from <code>traverse-runtime</code> 0.17.1. The connector does not clear <code>cancel</code>. Set it back to <code>false</code> before the next call. A flag that is already set fails the call before the guest starts. A flag set during the call fails with <code>cancelled</code> and message <code>model.execute cancelled mid-run</code>.</p>

  <h2>Node</h2>
  <p>Node has no <code>Worker</code>, and <code>createWorker: null</code> is the same path. The guest runs on the calling thread. The host checks the signal before the call and again after the guest returns, then reports <code>cancelled</code>. A guest that never returns stays running.</p>

  <h2>Which release</h2>
  <p>This is Decisions 104 and 131, Spec 138 0.16.1 and 0.16.2, <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1582">traverse#1582</a>, in the v0.17.0 changes. v0.17.1 is the product release. npm <code>traverse-embedder-web@0.17.0</code> already has the browser Worker. crates.io has no <code>traverse-runtime</code> 0.17.0, so the wasmtime path starts at 0.17.1. Swift, Kotlin, and .NET already stop <code>wasmi</code> guests between fuel slices. That API is older than this release. No host is Certified under Spec 529.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
